synopsis: Idle GC and deadlock detection in the non-threaded RTS now behave the same as in the threaded RTS
description: {
Idle GC now works in the non-threaded RTS and is enabled by default.

Deadlock detection in the non-threaded RTS can now detect partial
deadlocks where some but not all threads are deadlocked, whereas
previously it could only detect system deadlocks where all threads
were deadlocked.

Because (partial) deadlock detection runs as part of idle GC, this
change also enables the idle GC by default in the non-threaded RTS.
It was already enabled by default in the threaded RTS.

In addition, the non-threaded RTS can still detect system deadlocks
without relying on idle GC. Thus there is no regression in deadlock
detection behaviour for the non-threaded RTS.

The user guide now documents that deadlock detection relies on idle GC,
and thus turning off idle GC disables deadlock detection (except for
system deadlock detection in the non-threaded RTS). This was true of
the threaded RTS in previous GHC releases but it was not previously
documented behaviour.

Internally, the idle GC and deadlock detection now follow the same
design and use mostly the same code between the non-threaded and
threaded RTS. Having a single design improves the comprehension and
maintainability of the RTS code.
}
